WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.410
When a secondary school student said his hot ice cream mix froze before the cold one, the class laughed and his teacher called it "your physics, Mpemba physics".

2
00:00:09.410 --> 00:00:17.540
Sixty years later the most careful measurements made on water did not vindicate the student — but laser-trapped glass beads and a single ion did.

3
00:00:17.540 --> 00:00:23.110
The answer to the question is neither yes nor no; the question itself was asked wrong.

4
00:00:31.310 --> 00:00:46.480
The science channel's own config (`podcast-science.json`: `"language": "en"`, `kaynak_kanal: bilim`) and its hard constraint make this an English translation of today's `bilim.txt`,

5
00:00:46.480 --> 00:00:54.130
not a new Turkish text — so I'm delivering it in English, with numbers spelled out (the `en` path skips the Turkish number normalization in `kanal-render.ps1:56`).

6
00:00:54.130 --> 00:01:04.820
In nineteen sixty-three, in the Tanga region of northern Tanzania, a group of students between thirteen and fourteen years old were making ice cream in the cookery room of Magamba Secondary School.

7
00:01:04.820 --> 00:01:14.070
The recipe was simple: boil the milk, stir in sugar, wait until it cools to room temperature, then put it in the freezing compartment of the refrigerator.

8
00:01:14.070 --> 00:01:20.920
There was only room for a few containers in that compartment, and every week the students went through the same race for space.

9
00:01:20.920 --> 00:01:25.530
That day a student named Erasto Mpemba decided not to wait his turn.

10
00:01:25.530 --> 00:01:31.580
He mixed the boiling milk with sugar and put the container straight into the freezer while it was still steaming.

11
00:01:31.580 --> 00:01:36.830
An hour and a half later, when the freezer was opened, Mpemba's mixture had frozen.

12
00:01:36.830 --> 00:01:40.320
The containers that had been cooled first were still liquid.

13
00:01:40.320 --> 00:01:43.010
This was not supposed to happen.

14
00:01:43.010 --> 00:01:52.900
To cool down, a hot body must first pass through the temperature of the cold body; by the time it gets there, you expect it to still be behind, or at best to have caught up.

15
00:01:52.900 --> 00:01:58.470
When Mpemba described what he had seen in his physics lesson, his teacher made fun of him.

16
00:01:58.470 --> 00:02:04.040
This was not physics, the teacher said; it was, at most, Mpemba's physics.

17
00:02:04.040 --> 00:02:06.730
His classmates laughed too.

18
00:02:06.730 --> 00:02:09.580
But Mpemba did not let it go.

19
00:02:09.580 --> 00:02:18.030
He asked about the method used by an ice cream seller in a nearby town, and the man told him he put the milk in hot, because that way it froze more quickly.

20
00:02:18.030 --> 00:02:26.000
A few years later, while Mpemba was studying at Mkwawa High School, a physicist came to the school from the university in Dar es Salaam.

21
00:02:26.000 --> 00:02:38.130
After Denis Osborne finished his talk, during the questions, Mpemba stood up and asked his question: if I take two equal volumes of water, bring one to thirty-five degrees and the other to one hundred degrees,

22
00:02:38.130 --> 00:02:45.140
and put them both in the freezer, why does the one at one hundred degrees freeze first?

23
00:02:45.140 --> 00:02:47.350
The hall laughed again.

24
00:02:47.350 --> 00:02:49.240
Osborne did not.

25
00:02:49.240 --> 00:02:54.810
He said he did not know the answer, and decided to try it in the laboratory when he got back.

26
00:02:54.810 --> 00:02:58.140
He asked a technician to set up the experiment.

27
00:02:58.140 --> 00:03:05.150
When the technician brought him the results, he was surprised: under some conditions, hot water really did freeze first.

28
00:03:05.150 --> 00:03:17.600
Osborne repeated the experiment himself, refined the measurements, and in nineteen sixty-nine published a short paper, co-signed with Mpemba, in Physics Education, the journal put out by the Institute of Physics.

29
00:03:17.600 --> 00:03:24.930
From that day on, the oddity carried the surname of a schoolboy who had been laughed at in class: the Mpemba effect.

30
00:03:24.930 --> 00:03:28.740
The curious part is that the observation was not new.

31
00:03:28.740 --> 00:03:37.030
Aristotle, in his book on the phenomena of the weather, had written that people who wanted to cool water quickly first left it standing in the sun.

32
00:03:37.030 --> 00:03:45.160
Francis Bacon, in his work of sixteen twenty, Novum Organum, noted that lukewarm water freezes more readily than ice-cold water.

33
00:03:45.160 --> 00:03:48.010
Descartes made a similar remark.

34
00:03:48.010 --> 00:03:54.380
So the observation had been drifting around for centuries, had been written down, and had then been forgotten.

35
00:03:54.380 --> 00:04:04.910
Once modern physics had settled its concepts of temperature, heat and heat transfer, folk observations of this kind were no longer taken seriously, because the theory said it could not happen.

36
00:04:04.910 --> 00:04:09.520
What Mpemba did was not a discovery; it was an act of persistence.

37
00:04:09.520 --> 00:04:15.890
With nobody willing to agree with him, he pushed hard enough to turn his observation into a defensible question.

38
00:04:15.890 --> 00:04:18.740
He never became a physicist.

39
00:04:18.740 --> 00:04:28.630
He went to the wildlife management college at Mweka, worked as an officer in Tanzania's game and forestry administration, taught, and died in twenty twenty.

40
00:04:28.630 --> 00:04:34.040
His name entered the physics literature because, once in his life, he asked a question.

41
00:04:34.040 --> 00:04:37.850
But an effect having a name is not proof that it exists.

42
00:04:37.850 --> 00:04:45.500
And for the next fifty years, the Mpemba effect remained one of the most stubbornly elusive phenomena in physics.

43
00:04:45.500 --> 00:04:51.550
The problem was this: whoever ran the experiment, somebody else could not get the same result.

44
00:04:51.550 --> 00:04:55.520
Some researchers reported that hot water froze first.

45
00:04:55.520 --> 00:04:58.210
Some found no difference at all.

46
00:04:58.210 --> 00:05:02.020
Some found exactly the opposite.

47
00:05:02.020 --> 00:05:09.030
Trials carried out in the same laboratory, with the same equipment, in the same week, could contradict one another.

48
00:05:09.030 --> 00:05:15.560
That amounted to a lasting failure on the most basic test of whether a phenomenon is real: reproducibility.

49
00:05:15.560 --> 00:05:21.930
Over the years the proposed explanations grew into a list, and every one of them sounded plausible.

50
00:05:21.930 --> 00:05:30.540
Evaporation: boiling water evaporates rapidly in a freezer, loses part of its mass, and less water is left to freeze.

51
00:05:30.540 --> 00:05:41.070
This is a genuine effect and it can be measured in open containers; but when the containers were sealed the difference usually disappeared, and yet in some experiments it showed up in sealed containers too.

52
00:05:41.070 --> 00:05:48.240
Dissolved gases: boiled water holds less dissolved air, which can change how it conducts heat and how it freezes.

53
00:05:48.240 --> 00:05:53.170
Measurements showed that this difference was not large enough to account for the effect on its own.

54
00:05:53.170 --> 00:06:06.100
Convection: strong currents form inside hot water, the distribution of temperature inside the container is completely different from that in a cold one, the surface stays hotter, and heat loss from the surface speeds up.

55
00:06:06.100 --> 00:06:11.510
This was perhaps one of the strongest candidates, but it was not sufficient by itself.

56
00:06:11.510 --> 00:06:21.880
The layer of ice: if there is a thin coating of frost on the freezer shelf, the hot container melts the frost beneath it, sticks to the shelf and makes direct contact with the metal,

57
00:06:21.880 --> 00:06:26.330
while the cold container goes on sitting on an insulating bed of frost.

58
00:06:26.330 --> 00:06:35.420
This explained very well why the effect turns up in certain kitchen experiments, but what it explained was a property of the freezer shelf, not a property of water.

59
00:06:35.420 --> 00:06:40.190
And underneath all of them lay a more insidious problem: what does it mean to freeze?

60
00:06:40.190 --> 00:06:43.360
The appearance of the first ice crystal?

61
00:06:43.360 --> 00:06:46.690
All of the water dropping to zero degrees?

62
00:06:46.690 --> 00:06:50.340
The entire contents of the container turning solid?

63
00:06:50.340 --> 00:07:00.710
Water can pull a trick called supercooling: it can go below zero, sometimes down to minus ten degrees, while remaining liquid, and then freeze all at once when something triggers it.

64
00:07:00.710 --> 00:07:08.680
When that trigger happens is largely a matter of chance, depending on a speck of dust inside the container, a scratch, a vibration.

65
00:07:08.680 --> 00:07:18.090
In other words, which of two identical containers froze first was already close to a throw of the dice, quite independently of the effect anyone was trying to measure.

66
00:07:18.090 --> 00:07:29.260
In twenty twelve the Royal Society of Chemistry turned the question into a public competition, and the call for the best explanation drew more than twenty-two thousand entries from over one hundred countries.

67
00:07:29.260 --> 00:07:37.550
The winning submission came from Nikola Bregović, a chemist working in Zagreb, whose analysis combined convection and supercooling.

68
00:07:37.550 --> 00:07:44.880
But the competition itself looked rather like a confession: fifty years on, there was still no agreed explanation.

69
00:07:44.880 --> 00:07:48.050
The real blow came in twenty sixteen.

70
00:07:48.050 --> 00:07:56.340
Two fluid physicists, Henry Burridge and Paul Linden, working in London and Cambridge, came at the matter from an unusual direction.

71
00:07:56.340 --> 00:08:00.790
They began by asking what the precise definition of this effect was.

72
00:08:00.790 --> 00:08:07.640
The definition in the literature ran like this: water that starts hotter freezes before water that starts colder.

73
00:08:07.640 --> 00:08:14.010
They translated that into a measurable statement and looked for the point where the cooling curves of two samples crossed.

74
00:08:14.010 --> 00:08:17.340
Then they ran a large number of experiments.

75
00:08:17.340 --> 00:08:22.750
What they found was less about whether the effect existed than about how fragile the measurement was.

76
00:08:22.750 --> 00:08:30.880
The time difference observed between two samples was extremely sensitive to exactly where inside the container the thermocouple measuring the temperature was sitting.

77
00:08:30.880 --> 00:08:36.930
Moving the thermometer by one centimeter was enough to wipe out the measured difference, or to flip its sign.

78
00:08:36.930 --> 00:08:47.780
In other words, the difference that people had been reporting as the Mpemba effect for decades was, in most cases, of the same order of magnitude as the temperature distribution inside the container,

79
00:08:47.780 --> 00:08:51.110
the position of the sensor and the noise in the measurement.

80
00:08:51.110 --> 00:09:01.640
They published their results in Scientific Reports and reached this conclusion: under ordinary conditions there is no reliable, reproducible evidence that water freezes faster when it is hot.

81
00:09:01.640 --> 00:09:04.170
This looked like the end of the story.

82
00:09:04.170 --> 00:09:09.100
A centuries-old kitchen observation had evaporated under careful measurement.

83
00:09:09.100 --> 00:09:12.750
But at exactly this point the question changed hands.

84
00:09:12.750 --> 00:09:16.880
Physicists stopped looking at water and looked at the question itself.

85
00:09:16.880 --> 00:09:23.090
You can ask the following question without any reference to water at all: is it possible, in principle,

86
00:09:23.090 --> 00:09:31.060
for a system setting out from a hot initial state to reach equilibrium faster than the same system setting out from a cold initial state?

87
00:09:31.060 --> 00:09:37.110
Posed that way, the issue is not when ice forms, but how a system relaxes towards equilibrium.

88
00:09:37.110 --> 00:09:48.440
We are used to representing the state of an object with a single number, its temperature; but the true state of a many-particle system is a probability distribution over the possible arrangements of those particles.

89
00:09:48.440 --> 00:09:56.570
Cooling is the journey of that distribution, in an abstract space, from one point towards another point corresponding to cold equilibrium.

90
00:09:56.570 --> 00:10:05.660
And just as on a map, a route starting from a distant point can take less time than a route starting from a nearby one, provided it lies along a more direct road.

91
00:10:05.660 --> 00:10:11.870
In twenty seventeen, Zhiyue Lu and Oren Raz turned this intuition into mathematics.

92
00:10:11.870 --> 00:10:22.880
As a system goes to equilibrium it does so as the sum of a series of components that decay at different rates, and the slowest-decaying component is the one that holds the process back over the long run.

93
00:10:22.880 --> 00:10:29.890
Lu and Raz showed that for a specially chosen initial state the contribution of that slowest component can be zero.

94
00:10:29.890 --> 00:10:36.420
The system then falls to equilibrium through the fast components alone, never using the slow road at all.

95
00:10:36.420 --> 00:10:46.950
This opened the door that a hot start needed in order to overtake a cold one, and on one side of the scales there was now not a kitchen anecdote but nonequilibrium statistical physics.

96
00:10:46.950 --> 00:10:49.480
Next came the demonstration.

97
00:10:49.480 --> 00:10:57.130
At Simon Fraser University in Canada, Avinash Kumar and John Bechhoefer set water and freezers aside entirely.

98
00:10:57.130 --> 00:11:01.900
Their experiment was a single micron-scale glass bead suspended in water.

99
00:11:01.900 --> 00:11:12.750
They placed the bead inside an optical trap built from laser light, and shaped the intensity distribution of that light so that the bead sat within an energy landscape with two wells, one deeper than the other.

100
00:11:12.750 --> 00:11:21.520
In such a landscape the bead moves back and forth between the wells at random and, over the long run, settles into a particular probability distribution.

101
00:11:21.520 --> 00:11:25.810
That distribution is what serves as the temperature of this tiny system.

102
00:11:25.810 --> 00:11:37.780
The experiment ran like this: start the bead off with distributions corresponding to different temperatures, then release them all into the same cold bath and measure how long each takes to settle into equilibrium.

103
00:11:37.780 --> 00:11:45.110
The path of a single bead is noisy, so they repeated the experiment thousands of times and gathered statistics.

104
00:11:45.110 --> 00:11:55.000
The result was published in twenty twenty in Nature: under certain initial conditions the system reached equilibrium many times faster than a system that started colder.

105
00:11:55.000 --> 00:12:04.410
What is more, this was not a difference barely picked out of the noise, but a speed-up that separated exponentially, exactly where the theory had said in advance it would be.

106
00:12:04.410 --> 00:12:12.860
In twenty twenty-two the same group measured the reverse as well: under certain conditions a system with a cold start can also get ahead while heating up.

107
00:12:12.860 --> 00:12:15.550
After that things moved quickly.

108
00:12:15.550 --> 00:12:24.320
In twenty twenty-four, in a quantum simulator built from trapped ions, a similar speed-up was observed in the return of quantum systems to their symmetries.

109
00:12:24.320 --> 00:12:33.570
The Mpemba effect is no longer studied as a quirk belonging to water, but as a general behaviour that can arise in a wide variety of systems with relaxation dynamics.

110
00:12:33.570 --> 00:12:37.700
So the honest answer to the question has two layers.

111
00:12:37.700 --> 00:12:46.150
In the freezer in your kitchen, you cannot count on hot water freezing before cold; careful measurements show that this is not a reproducible phenomenon,

112
00:12:46.150 --> 00:12:56.520
and that the differences people observe come mostly from details such as evaporation, frost, where the container is placed, the randomness of supercooling and the position of the sensor.

113
00:12:56.520 --> 00:13:03.370
But the physics behind the question, the question of whether a hot start can reach equilibrium sooner, is not a fake.

114
00:13:03.370 --> 00:13:10.220
It has been measured in a properly constructed system, it was calculated in advance, and it has a theory of its own.

115
00:13:10.220 --> 00:13:14.670
This is a kind of success that science rarely talks about.

116
00:13:14.670 --> 00:13:19.440
When an observation turns out to be wrong, the story usually ends there.

117
00:13:19.440 --> 00:13:29.330
Here the observation, at least in the form in which it was claimed, was not confirmed; and yet the question itself opened a door nobody had expected.

118
00:13:29.330 --> 00:13:37.140
If physicists today discuss shortening a system's path to equilibrium, or speeding up cooling through engineered initial conditions,

119
00:13:37.140 --> 00:13:47.190
then somewhere in the family tree of that discussion stands a secondary school student who opened a freezer door in nineteen sixty-three and saw that his own container had frozen.

120
00:13:47.190 --> 00:13:50.360
Mpemba was not proved right.

121
00:13:50.360 --> 00:13:53.210
But his question outlived its answer.

122
00:13:53.210 --> 00:13:56.860
Nöron Science'de bugünkü bölümün sonuna geldik.

123
00:13:56.860 --> 00:14:00.670
Yeni bir hikâyede yeniden buluşmak üzere.
